# The class and level of every hunk in aset-sublet.patch, for capstone/tests/port-effort.py.
# Derived from the three facts the patch header itself states as forcing every change, and the
# constructs it names under each. NOT written by the author of the port: it is a reading of that
# header, and the author should check it before the number is quoted.
#
#   "a capability cannot reach out of the object it was given for"  -> what used to be derived by
#   arithmetic becomes a stored field or an index, which is where the bookkeeping lives: metadata
#   "a revocation cannot spare part of what it covers"              -> the header leaves the pool
#   "a revoked chunk cannot be read or written"                     -> the free list leaves it
#
# A hunk whose driving change is a sublet or sub-pool primitive is hierarchy; a hunk whose driving
# change is where the bookkeeping now lives is metadata.

aset.c:49     hierarchy   allocator   pg_subpool.h, the level below arrives
aset.c:108    metadata    allocator   the block header leaves the block, ALLOC_BLOCKHDRSZ pinned at 80
aset.c:148    metadata    allocator   AllocFreeListLink and GetFreeListLink go, the link was in the chunk
aset.c:182    metadata    allocator   freelist becomes an index, pool and keeper become fields
aset.c:224    metadata    allocator   AllocBlockData, the in-block header, is gone
aset.c:247    metadata    allocator   ChunkGetBlock reads an index instead of walking back
aset.c:295    metadata    allocator   KeeperBlock is a field, not the header's address plus a constant
aset.c:418    metadata    allocator   the assertion moves to the header slot that now holds the context
aset.c:489    metadata    allocator   mem_allocated from KeeperSize, not from pointer distance
aset.c:504    hierarchy   allocator   pg_subpool_header and pg_subpool_create: the sub-pool is born
aset.c:538    hierarchy   allocator   the keeper block comes from the sub-pool
aset.c:610    metadata    allocator   mem_allocated from KeeperSize again
aset.c:631    metadata    allocator   keepersize is now read, not assert-only
aset.c:640    hierarchy   allocator   pg_subpool_reset: one revocation replaces the block walk
aset.c:703    hierarchy   allocator   the locals of the walk that is gone
aset.c:711    metadata    allocator   the keeper size derivation that is gone with it
aset.c:739    hierarchy   allocator   pg_subpool_destroy and header_free at context death
aset.c:760    hierarchy   allocator   the teardown loop is gone, one revocation covers it
aset.c:794    metadata    allocator   a slot index where a chunk pointer stood
aset.c:807    hierarchy   allocator   pg_subpool_block, with grow when the pool is short
aset.c:881    hierarchy   allocator   pg_subpool_carve hands out the chunk
aset.c:963    metadata    allocator   a slot index where a chunk pointer stood
aset.c:979    hierarchy   allocator   pg_subpool_carve on the other hand-out path
aset.c:1019   hierarchy   allocator   pg_subpool_block for an oversized block
aset.c:1118   metadata    allocator   the free list is walked by index, not through the chunk
aset.c:1201   metadata    allocator   the freeptr check goes with the in-block header
aset.c:1236   metadata    allocator   size from block->base; the CLOBBER_FREED_MEMORY wipe is gone
aset.c:1266   metadata    allocator   the slot comes from the chunk's header, not a link inside it
aset.c:1285   metadata    allocator   the free-list link is no longer written into the freed chunk
aset.c:1326   hierarchy   allocator   realloc's locals for the copy path
aset.c:1358   hierarchy   allocator   realloc above the limit becomes carve, copy, give: the one policy change
aset.c:1536   metadata    allocator   ChunkGetBlock again
aset.c:1570   metadata    allocator   the chunk's size comes from the side table
aset.c:1637   metadata    allocator   stats walk the free list by index and size blocks from base
aset.c:1700   metadata    allocator   AllocSetCheck sizes a block from base, not from its own address
aset.c:1745   metadata    allocator   AllocSetCheck walks chunks by index, it cannot form the pointers
